  "summary": "The Gustavian style (Swedish: Gustaviansk stil) is a Swedish furniture and interior design style that emerged in the late 18th century, primarily during the reign of King Gustav III of Sweden (1771–1792) and continued into the reign of his son, Gustav IV Adolf of Sweden.",
  "description": "The Gustavian style (Swedish: Gustaviansk stil) is a Swedish furniture and interior design style that emerged in the late 18th century, primarily during the reign of King Gustav III of Sweden (1771–1792) and continued into the reign of his son, Gustav IV Adolf of Sweden. It is a Swedish variant of French Neoclassicism, influenced by the Louis XVI style and elements of Rococo.\n\nThe style is marked by a return to classical ideals, with a focus on restrained decoration, symmetry, and proportion. The Gustavian style is often described as a reaction against the excesses of Rococo, seeking simplicity and elegance, while maintaining classical references.",
  "culturalContext": "The Gustavian style emerged during the reign of King Gustav III, who was instrumental in the development and dissemination of the style. After visiting Versailles in 1771, Gustav III became highly influenced by French Neoclassicism and sought to adopt elements of the Louis XVI style, but tailored to Swedish tastes and available materials. The king’s support of artists, architects, and craftsmen was key in the propagation of the style throughout Sweden during his reign. This period saw a strong cultural exchange between Sweden and France, and the king encouraged the development of Swedish art and architecture under these new influences.",
